Political Sentiment Analysis Project
Project Overview

This project analyzes political tweets to determine their sentiment — positive, negative, or neutral.
It uses TextBlob for initial sentiment analysis and Machine Learning (Random Forest) for prediction and classification.
The project is integrated with a Flask REST API, so you can send tweets via Postman and get instant sentiment predictions.

Project Structure
political_sentiment_project/
│
├── app.py                     → Flask web application
├── main.py                    → Local standalone script to run pipeline
│
├── data/
│   ├── political_tweets.csv   → Original tweet dataset (manual or downloaded)
│   └── cleaned_df.csv         → Cleaned and processed tweets
│
├── src/
│   ├── preprocessing.py       → Data loading and cleaning
│   ├── sentiment.py           → Sentiment scoring using TextBlob
│   ├── features.py            → Feature extraction (word counts, polarity, etc.)
│   ├── model.py               → Model training (Random Forest)
│   ├── visualization.py       → Sentiment plots and charts
│   ├── pipeline.py            → Orchestrates full ML pipeline
│   └── model_utils.py         → Save/load model utilities
│
├── outputs/
│   ├── new_tweet_predictions.csv → Predicted results for new tweets
│   ├── model.pkl                 → Saved trained model
│   └── label_encoder.pkl         → Saved label encoder
│
├── requirements.txt           → Python library dependencies
└── README.txt                 → Project documentation

⚙️ Technologies Used

Python 3.x

Flask – REST API framework

Pandas – Data handling

TextBlob – Sentiment polarity

Scikit-learn – ML model and pipeline

Matplotlib – Data visualization

Joblib – Model saving/loading

 Features

 Cleans raw tweets (removes hashtags, mentions, links)
 Detects sentiment (Positive / Negative / Neutral)
 Calculates “Buzz Score” (weighted by retweet count)
 Trains and saves a Random Forest classifier
 Provides REST API endpoints to analyze new tweets
Works seamlessly with Postman

🚀 How to Run the Project
1️⃣ Setup Environment

Open project folder in PyCharm.

Create and activate a virtual environment:

python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate


Install dependencies:

pip install -r requirements.txt

2️⃣ Run Flask App

Run the app with:

python app.py


You should see:

 * Running on http://127.0.0.1:5000

🔌 API Endpoints (use Postman)
1. Run Full Pipeline

Method: GET

URL: http://127.0.0.1:5000/run_pipeline

Response:

{
  "message": "Pipeline executed successfully!"
}

2. Predict Sentiment for Tweets

Method: POST

URL: http://127.0.0.1:5000/predict

Body → raw → JSON:

{
  "tweets": [
    "PM Modi announces new welfare scheme for farmers.",
    "Congress questions government’s transparency.",
    "People are hopeful about upcoming elections."
  ]
}


Response Example:

{
  "predictions": [
    {
      "tweet": "PM Modi announces new welfare scheme for farmers.",
      "predicted_sentiment": "positive"
    },
    {
      "tweet": "Congress questions government’s transparency.",
      "predicted_sentiment": "negative"
    },
    {
      "tweet": "People are hopeful about upcoming elections.",
      "predicted_sentiment": "positive"
    }
  ]
}

📊 Output Files

cleaned_df.csv → All processed tweets with sentiment & buzz score

new_tweet_predictions.csv → Predictions from the latest API request

model.pkl → Saved trained ML model

label_encoder.pkl → Encoded sentiment label mapping

